From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Paslawski <chris@paslawski.me>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:03:48 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] android: preserve Camera3 conversion and buffer correctness

Rebase the working Android adapter from TI
3becb16c1fa22d79a4d7eeb75a36fc895b4b7abf and the previous ThinkSmart
queue, not TI's ISP algorithms. Preserve Alessandro Fontana's Android
fixes and the local conversion, metadata and shared-buffer corrections.

Keep RGB-to-NV12/JPEG conversion, derived stream sizes, DMA-heap
backing, mapper-provided YUV layout, full-range YUV dataspace and
correct JPEG trailer placement. Preserve release-fence ownership,
shared-source lifetime and failed-frame errors, locking the guarded
post-processing map during failed-request cleanup. An unsupported YUV
conversion must complete only with an error.

Retain frame-duration requests, capability metadata, configured mounting
orientation and failed-provider rejection. Adapt spans, ValueNode YAML
parsing, renamed properties and optional orientation results to upstream
v0.8.0. Publish the full native PixelArrayArea size and the active
rectangle's left/top/width/height, which Android's Rect marshaler
converts into Java right/bottom edges. Preserve native active-area
offsets without adding them twice. Reject missing or inconsistent
geometry explicitly.

Allow pipeline-assigned allocation defaults during stream validation:
upstream simple reports Adjusted when assigning an initially unspecified
buffer count. Keep rejecting changed stream count, sizes, pixel formats,
sensor orientation, explicit color spaces or explicit buffer counts.
Report configuration rejection at error severity, and use the validated
buffer count for Android max_buffers and allocation.

Allow a per-camera min-frame-rate in camera_hal.yaml to bound the
advertised automatic FPS range, rejecting invalid values or a floor
above the camera's maximum YUV rate. The ThinkSmart configuration
selects 10-30 FPS, restoring the earlier port's approximately 100 ms
low-light frame budget through Android requests, not a global sensor or
IPA cap. The Camera app deliberately selects the lowest advertised
minimum compatible with 30 FPS; exposing 1-30 FPS with working VBLANK
control otherwise permits near-one-second frames. This is an explicit
Android responsiveness policy, not Lenovo calibration. Leave other
cameras' defaults and the physical/manual sensor limits unchanged.

The optional physical-camera and stream-combination callbacks must
report unsupported operations rather than return success without
implementing them. Do not import the old custom AWB, AGC, highlight,
hot-pixel or lookup-table policies.

The local source baseline was the ThinkSmart integration at
787b8bcd6f003e7dcf0fc186f3e2bc7c4a899630 with its existing applied
camera queue.

